WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
A reliable OpenRouter integration starts with account and key hygiene.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:11.000
OpenRouter authenticates API calls with a bearer token, usually supplied in an Authorization header.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:30.060
That token should be handled like any production secret: generated for a specific purpose, stored in a secret manager or deployment environment, never committed to source control, never pasted into support tickets, and never exposed to browser-side code unless the architecture is explicitly designed to protect it.

4
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.620
Cost control belongs in the same conversation as authentication.

5
00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:42.680
LLM calls can scale quickly because each request may include prompt tokens, output tokens, retries, and fallback attempts.

6
00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:48.060
A useful engineering habit is to decide where the spending boundary lives before traffic grows.

7
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:55.600
That may include per-environment keys, service-level budgets, request limits, alerts, and application-side throttling.

8
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:01.940
Even when a platform offers account controls, the application should still make abuse and runaway loops harder.

9
00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.580
Headers and metadata also deserve care.

10
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.720
OpenRouter supports common API conventions and may allow optional identifying headers for application attribution.

11
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.580
Use those fields deliberately.

12
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:24.520
They should help with legitimate accounting, monitoring, or product identification, not leak private customer names, internal project secrets, or sensitive prompt content.

13
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.720
In development, keep the integration boring on purpose.

14
00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:38.740
Load the key from an environment variable, fail closed when it is missing, redact it in logs, and use separate credentials for local testing, staging, and production.

15
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:44.040
These practices are ordinary, but they are what turn a convenient API into an auditable system.

16
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:50.140
Add alerts for unusual request volume so key misuse or looping jobs are discovered quickly.

17
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.240
This is especially important for shared services used by many features.
